ontracts for works above 37,500 and up to 40,000 euros, just under the legal limit. If those amounts were spread as in the band immediately below, 9.8 contracts would be expected. The observed-to-expected ratio is 3.8, with a 95% confidence interval of 2.4 to 5.9. December accounts for 8% of the contracts just under the limit, compared with 9% of all its works contracts. The awardee with the most contracts just under the limit, called awardee A here, has 4. Contract [1], for 37,602.36 euros, was awarded in December 2025.\nWhat it cannot show: The concentration of contracts near the legal limit does not indicate improper conduct. It could reflect market pricing, budget constraints, or separate needs. Statistical trends alone do not prove wrongdoing or mismanagement.\nWhat can be checked: Check the contract details for [1] to see what was purchased and the specific dates. Review awardee A’s contracts to identify patterns or repeated requests. Look at the distribution of contracts across different months to assess if December is an outlier.","origen_es":"modelo","origen_en":"modelo","modelo":"Qwen3-4B (Q4_K_M, llama.cpp)","regla":"obras","citas":["1oEfMg9YXB1t5r0ngvMetA%3D%3D","HB2TUB5asCI7u6%2B%2FR7DUoA%3D%3D","AB65FkEG5Twtm4eBPtV6eQ%3D%3D"],"generado":"2026-11-16"}}